4. Preprocessing and clock
Two timestamps govern every bond observation: the execution or quote time measures the move, and the report time decides when the signal can use it.
4.1 Clock
1. Bar length is 5 minutes. The bond day runs 08:00 to 17:00 New York. The equity day runs 09:30 to 16:00.
2. Tag each bar with a session bucket: pre-open, open (09:30 to 10:30), mid, close (15:00 to 16:00), post-close.
3. Align all inputs to the bar close by the last observation at or before the close.
4. In the backtest, use the report time to decide availability. Use the execution time to measure the move.
4.2 Issuer spread
1. Eligible bonds: senior unsecured, fixed coupon, 2 to 10 years to maturity, at least 250 million outstanding, not within 1 year of a call date, quoted on at least 80 percent of bars.
2. Compute OAS per bond from the quote mid. Do not use price. Price mixes the rates move with the credit move.
3. Aggregate to the issuer: duration-weighted mean of eligible bonds.
4. Floor the spread at 10 bp before the log. Compute Delta log s_t.
5. Mark a bar as updated when at least one eligible bond mid changed in the bar. Module A fits on updated bars only.
4.3 Equity and factors
1. Equity returns are mid-to-mid log returns at bar close.
2. Factor model per issuer: market (SPY), sector ETF, and size (IWM for issuers below 5 billion market cap). Betas from a 60-day rolling regression on daily returns, applied to bars.
3. r~^E is the residual of that model, cumulated over the horizon.
4.4 Scale
1. sigma_hat_t: EWMA of epsilon with a 2-day half-life.
2. Floor sigma_hat at 1.4826 times the 20-day MAD of epsilon.
4.5 Event filters
Remove these bars from estimation and suppress the signal on them in production:
• Earnings: from the close before the release to the close after.
• Rating actions: the day of the action and the next day.
• Macro releases (FOMC, CPI, NFP): 15 minutes before to 30 minutes after.
• Index rebalance days for the credit index and the equity index.
• Issuer corporate events (M&A, tender, exchange) when a news feed exists.
5. Module A: abnormal bond move
The abnormal move is the residual of the issuer spread change after the model removes the index, the rates, the issuer's own equity, and the bond's own lags.
\Delta \log s_{i,t} = \alpha_i + \beta_{m,i}\,\Delta \log \mathrm{CDX}_t + \beta_{r,i}\,\Delta y_t + \sum_{k=0}^{K} \phi_{k,i}\, r^E_{i,t-k} + \sum_{j=1}^{J} \psi_{j,i}\,\Delta \log s_{i,t-j} + \varepsilon_{i,t}
z_{i,t} = \frac{\varepsilon_{i,t}}{\hat\sigma_{i,t}}
Specification:
1. Fit per issuer on a rolling window of 120 trading days of updated bars. Refit daily after the close.
2. K = 6 equity lags (30 minutes). J = 3 own lags.
3. Estimator: Huber regression with ridge penalty lambda = 0.01 on the standardized regressors. Bond residuals have fat tails and OLS overweights the outliers.
4. The k = 0 term is mandatory. Without the contemporaneous equity return the residual keeps the part of the bond move that the stock caused, and Module B then measures the equity's own autocorrelation.
5. Keep a second residual z^raw from the same model without the equity terms. The gap z^raw - z reports how much of a move the equity already explained. Store both.
6. Abnormal event: the first bar where |z| >= c_z with c_z = 2.5. Apply a 60-minute refractory period per issuer. Record t_0 as the report time of the quote that produced the crossing.
7. Issuer-level z uses the issuer spread. Also compute z per bond for the curve-confirmation feature in Module C.
Acceptance checks for Module A:
• Residual autocorrelation at lags 1 to 6 is below 0.05 in absolute value for 90 percent of issuers.
• The share of events that coincide with a filtered event window is below 10 percent after the filters.
• The event rate per issuer is between 0.5 and 3 per week. Tune c_z within 2.0 to 3.0 to land in that range.
6. Module B: propagation estimation
Two predictive regressions on the event panel decide who adjusts after an abnormal move: the stock, the bond, or neither.
\tilde r^E_{i,t_0,t_0+h} = \gamma_h\, z_{i,t_0} + u_{i,t_0+h}
Z_{i,t_0,t_0+h} \equiv \frac{1}{\hat\sigma_{i,t_0}}\sum_{j=1}^{h} \varepsilon_{i,t_0+j} = \delta_h\, z_{i,t_0} + v_{i,t_0+h}
Specification:
1. Sample: all events from Module A outside the event filters, with the equity return measured from the equity mid at t_0 (availability time), not at the execution time.
2. Horizons h: 1, 3, 6, 12, 26, 78 bars (1 day), 2 days, 5 days. Events after 15:30 start the horizon at the next open.
3. Standard errors: two-way cluster by issuer and by date. Abnormal moves cluster on macro days and the horizons overlap.
4. Estimate gamma_h and delta_h per state bucket (Module C), then as the pooled conditional model gamma_h(x).
5. Diagnostic: the residual cross-correlation rho(k) = Corr(z_{i,t}, r~^E_{i,t+k}) for k from -12 to +12 bars on all bars, not only events. The lead claim needs rho(k) for k > 0 to differ from rho(-k).
